WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.979
NARRATOR: Artificial intelligence can be
organized around agents.

2
00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:09.666
NARRATOR: An agent receives percepts through
sensors and affects an environment through

3
00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:10.140
actuators.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.504
NARRATOR: A thermostat senses temperature and
controls heating; a game agent observes a

5
00:00:15.504 --> 00:00:17.155
board and selects moves.

6
00:00:17.155 --> 00:00:22.173
NARRATOR: Calling an action intelligent
requires a performance objective and

7
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:23.176
information assumptions.

8
00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:29.041
NARRATOR: A rational agent selects the action
expected to perform best given its percept

9
00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:34.068
history and knowledge, not one guaranteed to
succeed in every uncertain world.

10
00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:39.792
NARRATOR: The environment may be fully or
partially observable, deterministic or

11
00:00:39.792 --> 00:00:41.874
stochastic, static or changing.

12
00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:46.891
NARRATOR: These properties determine which
representations and algorithms are

13
00:00:46.891 --> 00:00:47.448
appropriate.

14
00:00:47.448 --> 00:00:51.860
NARRATOR: Begin by specifying the task
environment rather than choosing an AI

15
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.698
technique because it is fashionable.

16
00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:59.141
NARRATOR: A reflex agent maps the current
situation to an action, such as braking when

17
00:00:59.141 --> 00:01:00.556
an obstacle is close.

18
00:01:00.556 --> 00:01:05.710
NARRATOR: Reflexes can be fast and effective
when local rules cover the environment.

19
00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:11.286
NARRATOR: A planning agent represents possible
future states and evaluates action sequences

20
00:01:11.286 --> 00:01:12.215
before acting.

21
00:01:12.215 --> 00:01:16.771
NARRATOR: Planning becomes valuable when an
action's benefit depends on later consequences

22
00:01:16.771 --> 00:01:18.669
or when several routes compete.

23
00:01:18.669 --> 00:01:25.136
NARRATOR: It also has a cost: the state space
can grow rapidly, the model may be wrong, and

24
00:01:25.136 --> 00:01:27.291
the world may change during computation.

25
00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:32.764
NARRATOR: Many real systems combine
layers—fast safety reflexes around a slower

26
00:01:32.764 --> 00:01:33.261
planner.

27
00:01:33.261 --> 00:01:38.748
NARRATOR: The design question is not whether
planning is always smarter, but which

28
00:01:38.748 --> 00:01:42.546
consequences must be represented to meet the
performance objective.

29
00:01:42.674 --> 00:01:45.583
NARRATOR: Search requires a precise
formulation.

30
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:49.894
NARRATOR: A state contains the information
needed to choose future actions.

31
00:01:49.894 --> 00:01:52.624
NARRATOR: Actions specify available choices.

32
00:01:52.624 --> 00:01:55.442
NARRATOR: The transition model predicts the
next state.

33
00:01:55.442 --> 00:01:58.440
NARRATOR: Action cost measures the value
spent.

34
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:04.279
NARRATOR: The start state identifies the
initial condition, and a goal test recognizes

35
00:02:04.279 --> 00:02:04.728
success.

36
00:02:04.728 --> 00:02:10.841
NARRATOR: In route finding, a state may be a
location, actions may follow roads, and cost

37
00:02:10.841 --> 00:02:12.751
may represent distance or time.

38
00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:18.502
NARRATOR: Include too little state and the
model cannot predict consequences; include

39
00:02:18.502 --> 00:02:22.815
irrelevant detail and the search space becomes
needlessly large.

40
00:02:22.815 --> 00:02:27.480
NARRATOR: The formulation determines what
solutions mean before any algorithm explores

41
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:27.904
them.

42
00:02:27.904 --> 00:02:33.826
NARRATOR: A correct algorithm on the wrong
state representation solves the wrong problem

43
00:02:33.826 --> 00:02:34.282
efficiently.

44
00:02:34.418 --> 00:02:39.368
NARRATOR: The state space is a graph of
possible configurations and transitions.

45
00:02:39.368 --> 00:02:43.041
NARRATOR: A search algorithm builds a tree of
paths from the start.

46
00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:47.607
NARRATOR: The same state may appear multiple
times because different paths reach it.

47
00:02:47.607 --> 00:02:53.066
NARRATOR: Tree search can therefore revisit
cycles forever or repeat expensive work.

48
00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:58.892
NARRATOR: Graph search maintains an explored
set or best-known cost information to control

49
00:02:58.892 --> 00:02:59.788
repeated expansion.

50
00:02:59.788 --> 00:03:03.181
NARRATOR: But duplicate handling must match
the algorithm.

51
00:03:03.181 --> 00:03:08.181
NARRATOR: In cost-based search, reaching a
known state through a cheaper path may require

52
00:03:08.181 --> 00:03:09.253
updating the frontier.

53
00:03:09.253 --> 00:03:15.820
NARRATOR: A search node often stores a state,
parent, action, path cost, and depth so the

54
00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.283
final action sequence can be reconstructed.

55
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:22.773
NARRATOR: State equality and hashing are part
of the algorithm's correctness.

56
00:03:22.898 --> 00:03:28.008
NARRATOR: Breadth-first search uses a
first-in, first-out frontier and explores by

57
00:03:28.008 --> 00:03:28.473
depth.

58
00:03:28.473 --> 00:03:33.192
NARRATOR: With equal action costs, the first
goal found has the fewest actions.

59
00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:36.113
NARRATOR: Its frontier can require large
memory.

60
00:03:36.113 --> 00:03:41.151
NARRATOR: Depth-first search uses a stack and
pursues one branch before backtracking.

61
00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:46.893
NARRATOR: It can use less frontier memory but
may spend a long time down an unhelpful branch

62
00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.595
and does not generally find the shortest
solution.

63
00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:54.200
NARRATOR: Graph search and finite-state
assumptions affect completeness.

64
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.472
NARRATOR: Neither strategy understands action
cost beyond depth.

65
00:03:58.472 --> 00:04:03.447
NARRATOR: Choose according to the guarantee
the problem needs, not a slogan that one is

66
00:04:03.447 --> 00:04:03.779
faster.

67
00:04:03.779 --> 00:04:09.606
NARRATOR: A small example with unequal edge
costs exposes why fewest actions and least

68
00:04:09.606 --> 00:04:11.687
total cost are different objectives.

69
00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:16.426
NARRATOR: Uniform-cost search orders the
frontier by path cost g of n.

70
00:04:16.426 --> 00:04:21.796
NARRATOR: It may explore a deeper path before
a shallow one when the deeper path is cheaper.

71
00:04:21.796 --> 00:04:26.415
NARRATOR: With nonnegative costs and
appropriate graph-search handling, the first

72
00:04:26.415 --> 00:04:28.263
removed goal is optimal.

73
00:04:28.263 --> 00:04:33.582
NARRATOR: The algorithm needs a priority queue
and must track the best cost found for states.

74
00:04:33.582 --> 00:04:38.850
NARRATOR: A cheaper path to an existing
frontier state should replace or supersede the

75
00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:39.602
expensive one.

76
00:04:39.602 --> 00:04:44.298
NARRATOR: Uniform-cost search uses no estimate
of remaining distance, so it can expand many

77
00:04:44.298 --> 00:04:48.658
states in directions that are cheap so far but
far from a goal.

78
00:04:48.658 --> 00:04:53.024
NARRATOR: It provides a baseline for
understanding how informed search adds

79
00:04:53.024 --> 00:04:55.406
guidance without giving up cost reasoning.

80
00:04:55.538 --> 00:05:01.342
NARRATOR: A heuristic h of n uses problem
knowledge to estimate remaining cost.

81
00:05:01.342 --> 00:05:06.279
NARRATOR: Straight-line distance can guide
geographic routing because it is inexpensive

82
00:05:06.279 --> 00:05:08.523
and related to travel distance.

83
00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:13.887
NARRATOR: Greedy best-first search chooses the
state with smallest h, focusing on apparent

84
00:05:13.887 --> 00:05:16.776
closeness while ignoring the cost already
paid.

85
00:05:16.776 --> 00:05:21.865
NARRATOR: It can be fast but is not generally
optimal or complete in all settings.

86
00:05:21.865 --> 00:05:26.112
NARRATOR: A heuristic should be cheaper to
compute than the search work it saves.

87
00:05:26.112 --> 00:05:30.870
NARRATOR: Zero is always an uninformative
baseline for nonnegative costs.

88
00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:34.937
NARRATOR: Better heuristics distinguish
promising states while respecting the

89
00:05:34.937 --> 00:05:37.197
guarantees required by the algorithm.

90
00:05:37.197 --> 00:05:42.775
NARRATOR: Heuristic design is representation
design: it encodes a simplified view of the

91
00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:43.204
problem.

92
00:05:43.346 --> 00:05:50.036
NARRATOR: A star balances the cost accumulated
so far, g, with the heuristic estimate to the

93
00:05:50.036 --> 00:05:50.872
goal, h.

94
00:05:50.872 --> 00:05:55.324
NARRATOR: If h is zero, A star behaves like
uniform-cost search.

95
00:05:55.324 --> 00:05:59.558
NARRATOR: If g is ignored, behavior resembles
greedy search.

96
00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:03.449
NARRATOR: The sum estimates total solution
cost through a frontier node.

97
00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:09.201
NARRATOR: An admissible heuristic never
overestimates the true remaining optimal cost.

98
00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:14.254
NARRATOR: Consistency requires that the
heuristic at one state be no greater than

99
00:06:14.254 --> 00:06:17.364
one-step cost plus the heuristic at the
successor.

100
00:06:17.364 --> 00:06:22.626
NARRATOR: Under standard conditions, these
properties support optimality and efficient

101
00:06:22.626 --> 00:06:23.678
graph-search handling.

102
00:06:23.678 --> 00:06:28.966
NARRATOR: A star's memory use can still be
substantial, and a highly accurate heuristic

103
00:06:28.966 --> 00:06:30.477
may itself be expensive.

104
00:06:30.477 --> 00:06:34.941
NARRATOR: Guarantees depend on the exact
assumptions and implementation.

105
00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:38.649
NARRATOR: A returned path is only one
dimension of quality.

106
00:06:38.649 --> 00:06:43.831
NARRATOR: Completeness asks whether an
algorithm will find a solution when one exists

107
00:06:43.831 --> 00:06:45.027
under stated conditions.

108
00:06:45.027 --> 00:06:49.823
NARRATOR: Optimality asks whether the solution
minimizes the defined cost.

109
00:06:49.823 --> 00:06:55.654
NARRATOR: Time and space can be estimated by
states expanded and stored, often in terms of

110
00:06:55.654 --> 00:06:57.476
branching factor and solution depth.

111
00:06:57.476 --> 00:07:03.248
NARRATOR: Practical evaluation also measures
frontier size, duplicate handling, and

112
00:07:03.248 --> 00:07:04.402
heuristic computation.

113
00:07:04.402 --> 00:07:09.606
NARRATOR: Most importantly, a mathematically
optimal path in an inaccurate model may be

114
00:07:09.606 --> 00:07:11.608
poor in the real environment.

115
00:07:11.608 --> 00:07:16.608
NARRATOR: Costs, transitions, and goals must
represent what users actually value.

116
00:07:16.608 --> 00:07:21.843
NARRATOR: Search evaluation joins algorithmic
guarantees with model validation rather than

117
00:07:21.843 --> 00:07:24.223
treating them as separate worlds.

118
00:07:24.362 --> 00:07:27.934
NARRATOR: A complete search design answers
nine questions.

119
00:07:27.934 --> 00:07:31.952
NARRATOR: What performance objective defines
rational behavior?

120
00:07:31.952 --> 00:07:34.490
NARRATOR: What information does each state
contain?

121
00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:37.283
NARRATOR: Which actions and transitions are
possible?

122
00:07:37.283 --> 00:07:39.222
NARRATOR: What does path cost represent?

123
00:07:39.222 --> 00:07:41.531
NARRATOR: What are the start and goal
conditions?

124
00:07:41.531 --> 00:07:44.898
NARRATOR: Which frontier rule matches the
required guarantee?

125
00:07:44.898 --> 00:07:48.342
NARRATOR: How are repeated states and cheaper
paths handled?

126
00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:53.317
NARRATOR: If a heuristic is used, what does it
estimate and what properties can be justified?

127
00:07:53.317 --> 00:07:59.573
NARRATOR: Finally, how do time, memory,
solution quality, and model error behave on

128
00:07:59.573 --> 00:08:00.536
representative cases?

129
00:08:00.536 --> 00:08:05.026
NARRATOR: Artificial intelligence is not
produced by naming an algorithm.

130
00:08:05.026 --> 00:08:09.856
NARRATOR: It comes from connecting a clear
environment model to a decision process whose

131
00:08:09.856 --> 00:08:11.926
behavior can be explained and tested.
